    <h1>Android Security: Why a VPN Complements Your Antivirus</h1>
    <p class="article-intro">Many people think an antivirus is enough to protect their Android phone. It's true for some threats — but not all. A VPN and an antivirus protect against different threats. Here's how the two complement each other.</p>

    <h2>What an Antivirus Does on Android</h2>
    <p>An antivirus scans installed apps and downloaded files for malware, spyware, and other malicious software. It protects <strong>your device</strong> against local threats.</p>
    <p>What an antivirus doesn't do: it doesn't protect your network connection. It doesn't encrypt your traffic. It doesn't hide your IP. It doesn't protect you on a compromised public Wi-Fi.</p>

    <h2>What a VPN Does on Android</h2>
    <p>A VPN protects <strong>your connection</strong>, not your device. It encrypts all traffic leaving your phone before it reaches the network. Result:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Someone on the same public Wi-Fi can't intercept your data</li>
      <li>Your ISP or mobile operator doesn't see what you do online</li>
      <li>Sites you visit see the VPN server's IP, not yours</li>
      <li>Your DNS requests are encrypted — domains you visit aren't visible</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="article-highlight">
      <h3>Antivirus vs VPN: Two Complementary Layers</h3>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Antivirus</strong> — protects the device: malware, malicious apps, infected files</p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>VPN</strong> — protects the connection: public Wi-Fi, ISP surveillance, IP tracking, DNS</p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Android updates</strong> — patch operating system vulnerabilities</p>
      <p>All three together cover the main attack surfaces on mobile.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Situations Where VPN Is Especially Useful on Android</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Public Wi-Fi</strong> — hotel, café, airport, public transport: your connection is encrypted even on unsecured networks</li>
      <li><strong>Banking apps from abroad</strong> — the VPN gives you a French IP when needed</li>
      <li><strong>4G/5G connections</strong> — even on mobile data, your operator doesn't see your traffic</li>
      <li><strong>Apps that collect your IP</strong> — forums, online games, various services that log your real IP</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Speed on Android: A Decisive Factor</h2>
    <p>Many people abandon their Android VPN because it slows the phone too much — slow pages, buffering videos, sluggish apps. This is a server overload problem, not a VPN problem in general.</p>
    <p>VulcainVPN maintains a <strong>dedicated server with a limited subscriber count</strong>. On a decent 4G or 5G connection, you shouldn't notice a significant speed difference. The VPN runs in the background without degrading your experience.</p>

